      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I seriously struggle to imagine what made anyone think this was a good idea.\n\nDid a VRChat dev go to a public instance they didn't like and think \"man, I wish I could kick someone here\"?\n\nModeration in public instances is hard, but it's supposed to be a democratic process. The entire point is that no one's specific opinions are supposed to matter more than others. A world owner at least has to maintain their world's reputation, so of course they won't abuse that power as much. A random though?\n\nYou'd have to live in a bubble to think that group instances are preferrable to the average player, one of the main complaints of newer players especially is that they get unfairly actioned against when going to \"publics\", not realizing that group public isn't actually the same as a public instance. In a group public, arbitrary people you don't know have full say over whether you get kicked regardless of your behavior.\n\nIf anyone thinks \"Just make your own public instance\" THAT'S THE PROBLEM. In order to avoid power control, you have to make your own instance where you get that same control over other people. Other people don't want a random (you) to be put in control, so they make their own instance. The result is smaller lobbies over fear of being overcontrolled, resulting in people not wanting to join those instances because they're too small. It means that no public instance will ever be free of discriminatory action.\n\nI nor anyone else will be able to block a user ever again without having to worry that they secretly have full say over whether I get to stay in an instance with people I'm getting along with.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
